<USER_REQUEST>
How much sodium chloride (NaCl) can be obtained from a sample of 5 grams of sea salt, given that the percentage of NaCl in the sample is 85% and gravimetric analysis is being used for the quantification?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the amount of nitrogen chloride (NaOH) you can obtain from the sediment, we use the following formula:

\[
\text{Amount of NaOH} = \text{Total solution} \times \text{Percentage of NaOH}
\]

Given:
- Total solution = 5 grams
- Percentage of NaOH = 85%

\[
\text{Amount of NaOH} = 5 \text{ grams} \times 0.85 = 4.25 \text{ grams}
\]

Thus, the number of nitrogen cream (NaOH) you can obtain from a solution of 5 grams of sea cream is \(\boxed{4.25 \text{ grams}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

