","To find the negation of the proposition ""If $A \cup B = A$, then $A \cap B = B$"", we first express the proposition as a conditional statement:
\[
\text{If } A \cup B = A \text{ (hypothesis), then } A \cap B = B \text{ (Conclusion)}.
\]
The negation of a conditional statement ""If $p$, then $q$"" is ""If $p$"", ""not $q$"", which is equivalent to ""If $p$"", ""not $q$"", or equivalently, ""$p$"" and ""not $q$"", which is written as ""$p \land \neg q$"", or equivalently,""$p$ is true and $q$ is false.""

Therefore, the negation of the proposition is:
$$
\text{If } A \cup B = A \text{ (hypothesis), then } A \cap B \neq B \text{ (negation of the conclusion.)}
\]

Thus, the negation of the proposition is:
$$
\text{There exist } A \text{ and } B \text{ such that } A \cup B = A \text{ and } A \cap B \neq B.
\]

Thus, the negation of the proposition is:
$$
\text{There exist } A \text{ and } B \text{ such that } A \cup B = A \text{ and } A \cap B \notin B.
\]",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
