To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
\text{Weight} = \text{Weight} \times \text{distance} \times \sin(\theta)
\]

Where:
- \( \text{Weight} \) is the distance the object is pushed
- \( \text{distance} \) is the distance the object is pushed
- \( \theta \) is the angle of inclination

In this case:
- \( \text{Weight} = 60.0 \text{ N} \)
- \( \text{distance} = 10.0 \text{ m} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)

So, the weight is:

\[
\text{Weight} = 60.0 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times \sin(60^\circ)
\]

\[
\text{Weight} = 60.0 \text{ nm} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times 0.581
\]

\[
\text{Weight} = 34.06 \text{m}
\]

