\[
\text{C}_3 \text{M}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O} = 1 \text{ g-mol}
\]
\[
\text{C}_2 \text{H}_5 \text{O} = 1 \text{ g-mol}
\]

At equilibrium, the remaining amount of anhydrous acid is 0.333 g-mol. This means that the difference between the initial amount and the amount at equilibrium is the amount of the reaction:
\[
\text{C}_3 \text{M}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O} \text{ (equilibrium)} = 1 - 0.333 = 0.667 \text{ g-mol}
\]

Since the reaction is in the ratio of 1:1, the amount of the resulting effluent is:
\[
\text{C}_3 \text{m}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O} = 1 - 0.333 = 0.667 \text{ g-mol}
\]

The equilibrium concentration of the effluent is:
\[
\text{C}_2 \text{h}_5 \text{o} = 1 - 0.667 = 0.333 \text{ g-mol}
\]

The equilibrium concentration of the aqueous acid is:
\[
\text{C}_3 \text{m}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O} = 0.333 \text{ g-mol}
\]

The equilibrium concentration of the electrolyte is:
\[
\text{C}_3 \text{m}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O}_3 \text{O} = 0.667 \text{ g-mol}
\]

The equilibrium coefficient is:
